							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Xaanru is mostly correct.&nbsp; Bush did call Nagin, but only after the decision had already been made to make a mandatory evactuation.&nbsp; That being said... the city/state did have a plan they were supposed to follow in event of a major hurricane (like Katrina) hitting N.O.&nbsp; The plan wasn&#039;t followed, not even close.&nbsp; In fact, they had even run drills on this procedure and had different situations set up just to test the system... they KNEW where the flaws were and failed to correct them.&nbsp; <br /><br />As for federal assistance, it HAS to be requested by the governor of the state, and she didn&#039;t contribute shit to the situation.&nbsp; In fact, she DENIED access to Red Cross who was trying to deliver supplies to those in town.&nbsp; Why?&nbsp; Because she wanted the evacuation to continue, and said that delivering supplies would encourage people to stay.<br /><br />As for the levees... they knew they&#039;d never withstand a direct hit of a major hurricane.&nbsp; Plus, the Corps of Engineers had been doing a study since 2000 on those same levees.<br /><br />The simple fact is, if heads are going to roll, make sure its the right heads.&nbsp; Yes, a large number of people have a part in this giant clusterf*ck, but personally, I hold the Governor largely responsible for the lack of coordination and idiot moves that took place before and after the fact.</td>
